1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,176
This is ECE 358, video 21. We start Chapter 6, the link layer. This video covers slides 1 to 17.

2
00:00:10,626 --> 00:00:21,399
After this video, you can name the link-layer services, compute parity and CRC bits, and answer the question-bank Link Layer questions 1 to 19.

3
00:00:21,849 --> 00:00:28,036
First, some terms. Hosts and routers are both called nodes. This path has four nodes.

4
00:00:28,486 --> 00:00:31,089
Host A sends a datagram to host B.

5
00:00:31,539 --> 00:00:35,592
The datagram passes through two routers, R1 and R2.

6
00:00:36,042 --> 00:00:41,717
The communication channels that connect adjacent nodes along the path are called links.

7
00:00:42,167 --> 00:00:46,220
A link can be wireless. Here, the first link is WiFi.

8
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:51,043
A link can be wired. The second link is a point-to-point wire.

9
00:00:51,493 --> 00:00:55,973
A link can also be a LAN. The third link is an Ethernet LAN.

10
00:00:56,423 --> 00:00:58,493
The layer 2 packet is a frame.

11
00:00:58,943 --> 00:01:02,228
The yellow header and trailer belong to the link layer.

12
00:01:02,678 --> 00:01:07,286
The purple part is the IP datagram. The frame encapsulates it.

13
00:01:07,736 --> 00:01:15,501
The link layer has one job. It moves a datagram from one node to the physically adjacent node, over one link.

14
00:01:15,951 --> 00:01:21,199
So a datagram from A to B crosses three links: three hops, one at a time.

15
00:01:21,649 --> 00:01:27,836
The same datagram travels over every link of the path. The network layer does not change it.

16
00:01:28,286 --> 00:01:36,926
But each link can use a different link protocol. The first link uses WiFi. It puts the datagram in a WiFi frame.

17
00:01:37,376 --> 00:01:41,920
The second link uses a point-to-point protocol, with its own frame.

18
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:46,061
The last link uses Ethernet, with an Ethernet frame.

19
00:01:46,511 --> 00:01:55,343
Each link protocol provides different services. For example, one protocol may provide reliable data transfer over its link.

20
00:01:55,793 --> 00:01:57,691
Another protocol may not.

21
00:01:58,141 --> 00:02:04,605
The slide gives a travel example: a trip from Princeton to Lausanne by limo, plane and train.

22
00:02:05,055 --> 00:02:07,125
The tourist is the datagram.

23
00:02:07,575 --> 00:02:10,945
Each transport segment is a communication link.

24
00:02:11,395 --> 00:02:14,851
The transportation mode is the link-layer protocol.

25
00:02:15,301 --> 00:02:18,352
The travel agent is the routing algorithm.

26
00:02:18,802 --> 00:02:24,818
The first service is framing. The link layer gets an IP datagram from the network layer.

27
00:02:25,268 --> 00:02:31,561
It encapsulates the datagram in a frame. First, it adds a header in front of the datagram.

28
00:02:32,011 --> 00:02:35,062
One header field is the destination address.

29
00:02:35,512 --> 00:02:38,221
Another header field is the source address.

30
00:02:38,671 --> 00:02:40,762
A third header field is the type.

31
00:02:41,212 --> 00:02:48,508
It also adds a trailer at the end. The trailer carries the error checking code, for example a CRC.

32
00:02:48,958 --> 00:02:53,950
The second service is link access. Here, three nodes share one medium.

33
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:57,088
Node 2 sends a frame on the shared medium.

34
00:02:57,538 --> 00:03:04,237
The protocol must find the best time to send, so that the frame reaches the destination with no collision.

35
00:03:04,687 --> 00:03:10,127
The addresses in the frame header are MAC addresses, also called hardware addresses.

36
00:03:10,577 --> 00:03:16,401
MAC means medium access control. A MAC address is different from an IP address.

37
00:03:16,851 --> 00:03:21,523
The third service is reliable delivery between adjacent nodes.

38
00:03:21,973 --> 00:03:32,127
We already know how to do this. Use the tools of Chapter 3: stop-and-wait, or a pipelined protocol with sequence numbers, ACKs and timeouts.

39
00:03:32,577 --> 00:03:42,604
Whether a link uses it depends on the bit error rate, B E R. The B E R is the number of bit errors divided by the number of bits sent.

40
00:03:43,054 --> 00:03:50,222
A B E R of 10 to the power minus 12 means 1 bit error per 10 to the power 12 bits.

41
00:03:50,672 --> 00:03:56,816
Ethernet has a B E R of 10 to the power minus 12 or less. Errors are very rare.

42
00:03:57,266 --> 00:04:01,874
So Ethernet does not use reliable delivery at the link level.

43
00:04:02,324 --> 00:04:08,831
WiFi has a B E R from 10 to the power minus 6 to 10 to the power minus 4.

44
00:04:09,281 --> 00:04:12,289
So WiFi uses reliable delivery.

45
00:04:12,739 --> 00:04:24,344
An example that is not on the slides. A 1500-byte frame has 1500 times 8, which is 12 000 bits.

46
00:04:24,794 --> 00:04:38,917
At a B E R of 10 to the power minus 4, the frame has 12 000 times 10 to the power minus 4 bit errors. That is 1 point 2 bit errors per frame, on average.

47
00:04:39,367 --> 00:04:52,913
At 10 to the power minus 12, it is 12 000 times 10 to the power minus 12. That is only 1 point 2 times 10 to the power minus 8 bit errors per frame.

48
00:04:53,363 --> 00:04:58,462
The slide asks: why use both link-level and end-to-end reliability?

49
00:04:58,912 --> 00:05:02,496
The link level fixes errors on one bad link quickly.

50
00:05:02,946 --> 00:05:11,629
But datagrams are also lost inside routers, when a buffer is full. Only TCP, end to end, recovers from that.

51
00:05:12,079 --> 00:05:18,628
The fourth service is flow control. It is pacing between adjacent sending and receiving nodes.

52
00:05:19,078 --> 00:05:21,425
The receiver sets the pace of the sender.

53
00:05:21,875 --> 00:05:24,243
Ethernet has no flow control.

54
00:05:24,693 --> 00:05:27,807
WiFi has flow control, with stop-and-wait.

55
00:05:28,257 --> 00:05:34,337
The fifth service is error detection. Signal attenuation and noise cause bit errors.

56
00:05:34,787 --> 00:05:38,563
The receiver detects the error with the check bits in the trailer.

57
00:05:39,013 --> 00:05:44,837
What happens next depends on the link. A WiFi receiver signals a retransmission.

58
00:05:45,287 --> 00:05:51,858
An Ethernet receiver drops the frame. Then TCP recovers the lost segment end to end.

59
00:05:52,308 --> 00:05:59,903
The sixth service is error correction. The receiver identifies and corrects the bit errors without a retransmission.

60
00:06:00,353 --> 00:06:05,793
4G and LTE networks do this with Forward Error Correction, F E C.

61
00:06:06,243 --> 00:06:11,320
The Internet model with TCP, IP and Ethernet does not use it.

62
00:06:11,770 --> 00:06:21,711
The last service on slide 8 is the duplex mode. On a half-duplex link, the nodes at both ends can transmit, but not at the same time.

63
00:06:22,161 --> 00:06:26,919
They send one at a time. In the picture, A sends, then B, then A again.

64
00:06:27,369 --> 00:06:29,630
The dashed boxes are idle time.

65
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:34,816
On a full-duplex link, both end nodes can transmit at the same time.

66
00:06:35,266 --> 00:06:37,250
Both lanes are always busy.

67
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:46,191
Count the frames in the same 3 frame times. The half-duplex link carries 3 frames. The full-duplex link carries 6.

68
00:06:46,641 --> 00:06:52,465
6 divided by 3 is 2. So a full-duplex link carries twice the total data.

69
00:06:52,915 --> 00:06:56,392
Where is the link layer implemented? Here is a host.

70
00:06:56,842 --> 00:06:58,997
It has a CPU and memory.

71
00:06:59,447 --> 00:07:01,239
They connect to the host bus.

72
00:07:01,689 --> 00:07:07,065
The application, transport and network layers run on the CPU, in software.

73
00:07:07,515 --> 00:07:11,035
The link layer is in each and every host and router.

74
00:07:11,485 --> 00:07:16,775
It is implemented in the network interface card, the N I C, or on a chip.

75
00:07:17,225 --> 00:07:20,916
Examples are an Ethernet card or a WiFi chip.

76
00:07:21,366 --> 00:07:25,483
The N I C implements the link layer and the physical layer.

77
00:07:25,933 --> 00:07:30,947
The N I C attaches to the host's system bus, for example P C I.

78
00:07:31,397 --> 00:07:36,005
So the link layer is a combination of hardware, software and firmware.

79
00:07:36,455 --> 00:07:43,665
Now two interfaces communicate. On each side, the link controller in the N I C connects to the link.

80
00:07:44,115 --> 00:07:48,958
On the sending side, the network layer gives a datagram to the N I C.

81
00:07:49,408 --> 00:07:53,227
The sending side encapsulates the datagram in a frame.

82
00:07:53,677 --> 00:07:56,599
It adds the error checking bits in the trailer.

83
00:07:57,049 --> 00:08:02,340
If the link uses them, it also does reliable data transfer and flow control.

84
00:08:02,790 --> 00:08:05,009
Then it sends the frame over the link.

85
00:08:05,459 --> 00:08:09,256
The receiving side gets the frame. It looks for errors.

86
00:08:09,706 --> 00:08:14,997
If the link uses them, it also does reliable data transfer and flow control.

87
00:08:15,447 --> 00:08:19,671
Then it extracts the datagram and passes it up to the network layer.

88
00:08:20,121 --> 00:08:27,417
How does a receiver detect bit errors? D is the data that the check protects. It can include header fields.

89
00:08:27,867 --> 00:08:33,691
E D C is a set of error detection and correction bits. These are redundant bits.

90
00:08:34,141 --> 00:08:36,637
The sender computes E D C from D.

91
00:08:37,087 --> 00:08:41,140
Then it sends D and E D C over a link that can flip bits.

92
00:08:41,590 --> 00:08:45,089
The receiver gets D prime and E D C prime.

93
00:08:45,539 --> 00:08:48,504
Here, one bit of D changed: the third bit.

94
00:08:48,954 --> 00:08:54,799
The receiver computes the check again on D prime, and compares it with E D C prime.

95
00:08:55,249 --> 00:08:58,663
If all bits are OK, it passes the datagram up.

96
00:08:59,113 --> 00:09:01,459
Otherwise, an error is detected.

97
00:09:01,909 --> 00:09:08,736
Error detection is not 100 percent reliable. The protocol may miss some errors, but rarely.

98
00:09:09,186 --> 00:09:13,687
A larger E D C field gives better detection and correction.

99
00:09:14,137 --> 00:09:25,316
The slide gives a small example: one third F E C. Each data bit gets 2 E D C bits, so each bit is sent 3 times: 1, 1, 1.

100
00:09:25,766 --> 00:09:30,267
On the link, one copy flips. The receiver gets 1, 0, 1.

101
00:09:30,717 --> 00:09:38,227
The receiver takes the majority. Two of the three bits are 1, so the bit was 1. The receiver corrects the error.

102
00:09:38,677 --> 00:09:45,525
The simplest check is a single parity bit. The sender has d data bits. Here, d is 16.

103
00:09:45,975 --> 00:09:54,828
The sender adds one parity bit. The slide uses odd parity: the total number of 1s, parity bit included, must be odd.

104
00:09:55,278 --> 00:09:58,926
Count the 1s in the data. There are 9. That is odd.

105
00:09:59,376 --> 00:10:04,645
9 is already odd, so the parity bit is 0, and the total stays odd.

106
00:10:05,095 --> 00:10:08,402
Now one bit flips on the link, from 0 to 1.

107
00:10:08,852 --> 00:10:17,748
The receiver counts 9 plus 1, which is 10 ones. That is even, so the odd parity check fails. The receiver detects the error.

108
00:10:18,198 --> 00:10:21,270
Now two bits flip, both from 0 to 1.

109
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:29,507
The receiver counts 9 plus 2, which is 11 ones. That is odd, so the check passes. The two errors are missed.

110
00:10:29,957 --> 00:10:36,997
So single-bit parity detects a single bit error. In fact, it detects any odd number of errors.

111
00:10:37,447 --> 00:10:41,628
It cannot tell which bit is wrong, so it cannot correct the error.

112
00:10:42,078 --> 00:10:47,774
With even parity, the sender chooses the bit so that the total number of 1s is even.

113
00:10:48,224 --> 00:10:55,712
Two-dimensional parity can also correct an error. The slide uses 15 bits of D, with even parity.

114
00:10:56,162 --> 00:11:00,813
Organize the data bits as a matrix: 3 rows of 5 bits.

115
00:11:01,263 --> 00:11:08,623
Compute one parity bit for each row. Row 1 has three 1s. Three is odd, so its parity bit is 1.

116
00:11:09,073 --> 00:11:13,766
Row 2 has four 1s. Four is even, so its parity bit is 0.

117
00:11:14,216 --> 00:11:17,651
Row 3 has three 1s, so its parity bit is 1.

118
00:11:18,101 --> 00:11:24,287
Then compute one parity bit for each column. Column 1 has two 1s, so its bit is 0.

119
00:11:24,737 --> 00:11:27,383
Column 2 has two 1s: bit 0.

120
00:11:27,833 --> 00:11:30,478
Column 3 has three 1s: bit 1.

121
00:11:30,928 --> 00:11:33,659
Column 4 has two 1s: bit 0.

122
00:11:34,109 --> 00:11:36,775
Column 5 has one 1: bit 1.

123
00:11:37,225 --> 00:11:43,625
The corner bit is the parity of the column parities. They have two 1s, so the corner bit is 0.

124
00:11:44,075 --> 00:11:51,286
The sender transmits D, then the parity bits, in the slide's order: row parities, corner, column parities.

125
00:11:51,736 --> 00:11:56,792
On the slide, the second received frame has one flipped bit. It is red here.

126
00:11:57,242 --> 00:12:06,181
The receiver checks every row. Row 2 now has three 1s, and its parity bit is 0. The total is odd, so row 2 fails.

127
00:12:06,631 --> 00:12:15,335
It checks every column. Column 2 now has one 1, and its parity bit is 0. The total is odd, so column 2 fails.

128
00:12:15,785 --> 00:12:23,635
The error is where the bad row and the bad column cross: row 2, column 2. The receiver flips that bit back.

129
00:12:24,085 --> 00:12:28,395
So two-dimensional parity detects and corrects a single bit error.

130
00:12:28,845 --> 00:12:35,693
The Internet checksum is a review from Chapter 3. Its goal is to detect flipped bits in a segment.

131
00:12:36,143 --> 00:12:45,657
The sender treats the UDP segment, with the UDP header fields and the IP addresses, as a sequence of 16-bit integers.

132
00:12:46,107 --> 00:12:52,785
The checksum is the one's complement sum of these words. Here is an optional example with two words.

133
00:12:53,235 --> 00:13:00,424
Add them like normal binary numbers. The result has 17 bits: there is a carry out of the top bit.

134
00:13:00,874 --> 00:13:04,863
Wraparound: take this carry away, and add it to the lowest bit.

135
00:13:05,313 --> 00:13:07,639
The result is the 16-bit sum.

136
00:13:08,089 --> 00:13:13,166
Then flip every bit. This is the one's complement. The result is the checksum.

137
00:13:13,616 --> 00:13:19,483
The receiver computes the checksum of the received segment and compares it with the checksum field.

138
00:13:19,933 --> 00:13:23,175
If they are not equal, an error is detected.

139
00:13:23,625 --> 00:13:28,468
If they are equal, no error is detected. But there may still be errors.

140
00:13:28,918 --> 00:13:36,961
Check the example. Each checksum bit is the flip of the sum bit above it. So the sum plus the checksum is sixteen 1s.

141
00:13:37,411 --> 00:13:47,224
A cyclic redundancy check, or CRC, is a more powerful error detection code. Think of the data bits D as a binary number.

142
00:13:47,674 --> 00:13:52,005
G is a bit pattern of r plus 1 bits, called the generator.

143
00:13:52,455 --> 00:13:56,188
G is public: the sender and the receiver both know it.

144
00:13:56,638 --> 00:14:07,646
The sender makes room for r CRC bits. It shifts D left by r bits. That is D times 2 to the power r: D followed by r zeros.

145
00:14:08,096 --> 00:14:13,323
Then it XORs R, the r CRC bits, into the r zero bits.

146
00:14:13,773 --> 00:14:16,077
The result is D followed by R.

147
00:14:16,527 --> 00:14:25,444
The sender chooses R so that this bit pattern, D followed by R, is exactly divisible by G, in mod 2 arithmetic.

148
00:14:25,894 --> 00:14:29,137
The receiver divides the received bits by G.

149
00:14:29,587 --> 00:14:33,299
If the remainder is not zero, it detects an error.

150
00:14:33,749 --> 00:14:38,698
A CRC detects all burst errors of fewer than r plus 1 bits.

151
00:14:39,148 --> 00:14:46,593
Ethernet and 802.11 WiFi use a CRC, with r equal to 32.

152
00:14:47,043 --> 00:14:56,131
To compute R, we need mod 2 arithmetic. Each bit is added on its own, with no carries. This is the XOR operation.

153
00:14:56,581 --> 00:14:58,736
0 XOR 0 is 0.

154
00:14:59,186 --> 00:15:03,367
0 XOR 1 is 1, and 1 XOR 0 is 1.

155
00:15:03,817 --> 00:15:06,825
1 XOR 1 is 0. There is no carry.

156
00:15:07,275 --> 00:15:16,683
For example, 1 0 1 1 XOR 1 0 0 1 is 0 0 1 0. Each column is computed on its own.

157
00:15:17,133 --> 00:15:23,832
In mod 2, there are no borrows either. So addition and subtraction are the same operation: XOR.

158
00:15:24,282 --> 00:15:32,986
Now the slide's derivation. We want D times 2 to the power r, XOR R, to be a multiple of G: n times G.

159
00:15:33,436 --> 00:15:43,377
XOR both sides with R. Because R XOR R is zero, D times 2 to the power r equals n G XOR R.

160
00:15:43,827 --> 00:15:49,182
So if we divide D times 2 to the power r by G, the remainder is R.

161
00:15:49,632 --> 00:15:54,432
And D times 2 to the power r is D followed by r zeros.

162
00:15:54,882 --> 00:16:01,538
Here is the example of slide 17. D is 1 0 1 1 1 0, six bits.

163
00:16:01,988 --> 00:16:08,025
G is 1 0 0 1, four bits. So r plus 1 is 4, and r is 3.

164
00:16:08,475 --> 00:16:14,257
Append r, which is 3, zeros to D. This is D times 2 to the power 3.

165
00:16:14,707 --> 00:16:18,355
Now divide by G, with mod 2 long division.

166
00:16:18,805 --> 00:16:25,525
Take the first four bits: 1 0 1 1. The leading bit is 1, so the quotient bit is 1.

167
00:16:25,975 --> 00:16:33,740
XOR 1 0 1 1 with G, 1 0 0 1. The result is 0 0 1 0.

168
00:16:34,190 --> 00:16:45,113
Drop the leading 0 and bring down the next bit, 1. The bits under G are now 0 1 0 1. The leading bit is 0, so the quotient bit is 0.

169
00:16:45,563 --> 00:16:51,301
XOR with four zeros. The bits do not change: 0 1 0 1.

170
00:16:51,751 --> 00:17:02,631
Drop the leading 0 and bring down the next bit, 0. The bits under G are now 1 0 1 0. The leading bit is 1, so the quotient bit is 1.

171
00:17:03,081 --> 00:17:10,889
XOR 1 0 1 0 with G, 1 0 0 1. The result is 0 0 1 1.

172
00:17:11,339 --> 00:17:22,369
Drop the leading 0 and bring down the next bit, 0. The bits under G are now 0 1 1 0. The leading bit is 0, so the quotient bit is 0.

173
00:17:22,819 --> 00:17:28,387
XOR with four zeros. The bits do not change: 0 1 1 0.

174
00:17:28,837 --> 00:17:39,695
Drop the leading 0 and bring down the next bit, 0. The bits under G are now 1 1 0 0. The leading bit is 1, so the quotient bit is 1.

175
00:17:40,145 --> 00:17:47,975
XOR 1 1 0 0 with G, 1 0 0 1. The result is 0 1 0 1.

176
00:17:48,425 --> 00:17:59,305
Drop the leading 0 and bring down the next bit, 0. The bits under G are now 1 0 1 0. The leading bit is 1, so the quotient bit is 1.

177
00:17:59,755 --> 00:18:07,563
XOR 1 0 1 0 with G, 1 0 0 1. The result is 0 0 1 1.

178
00:18:08,013 --> 00:18:14,818
There are no more bits to bring down. The remainder is the last 3 bits: R equals 0 1 1.

179
00:18:15,268 --> 00:18:19,577
The quotient is 1 0 1 0 1 1. We do not need it.

180
00:18:20,027 --> 00:18:26,513
The sender transmits D followed by R: 1 0 1 1 1 0, 0 1 1.

181
00:18:26,963 --> 00:18:39,187
The receiver divides the 9 received bits by G, 1 0 0 1, with the same long division. If the remainder is 0 0 0, it accepts the frame.

182
00:18:39,637 --> 00:18:47,743
If no bit changed, the received bits are D followed by R: 1 0 1 1 1 0 0 1 1.

183
00:18:48,193 --> 00:18:52,993
The remainder is 0 0 0. The receiver accepts the frame.

184
00:18:53,443 --> 00:18:56,003
Now one bit flips: the third bit.

185
00:18:56,453 --> 00:19:02,363
The remainder is 0 0 1. It is not zero, so the receiver detects the error.

186
00:19:02,813 --> 00:19:07,591
Now three adjacent bits flip. This is a burst error of 3 bits.

187
00:19:08,041 --> 00:19:13,076
The remainder is 1 1 1. It is not zero, so the error is detected.

188
00:19:13,526 --> 00:19:22,209
Now the error bits are 1 0 0 1, the bits of G, shifted left by 3. The error is a multiple of G.

189
00:19:22,659 --> 00:19:30,211
So the received word is still a multiple of G. The remainder is 0 0 0, and the error is missed.

190
00:19:30,661 --> 00:19:40,325
This matches slide 16. With r equal to 3, a CRC detects every burst of fewer than r plus 1, which is 4 bits.

191
00:19:40,775 --> 00:19:45,105
A 4-bit burst that equals G can be missed, but this is rare.

192
00:19:45,555 --> 00:19:59,785
Now a practice question. It is not from the question bank, but exams ask this type. D is 1 0 1 1 0 1 and G is 1 1 0 1. Find R. Pause the video and try it.

193
00:20:00,235 --> 00:20:05,867
G has 4 bits, so r is 3. Append three zeros, and divide by G.

194
00:20:06,317 --> 00:20:15,895
The first window is 1 0 1 1. It starts with 1, so the quotient bit is 1. XOR with G gives 0 1 1 0.

195
00:20:16,345 --> 00:20:26,287
Bring down a bit: 1 1 0 0. It starts with 1, so the quotient bit is 1. XOR with G gives 0 0 0 1.

196
00:20:26,737 --> 00:20:36,635
Bring down a bit: 0 0 1 1. It starts with 0, so the quotient bit is 0. XOR with zeros: 0 0 1 1.

197
00:20:37,085 --> 00:20:47,027
Bring down a bit: 0 1 1 0. It starts with 0, so the quotient bit is 0. XOR with zeros: 0 1 1 0.

198
00:20:47,477 --> 00:20:57,418
Bring down a bit: 1 1 0 0. It starts with 1, so the quotient bit is 1. XOR with G gives 0 0 0 1.

199
00:20:57,868 --> 00:21:08,065
Bring down a bit: 0 0 1 0. It starts with 0, so the quotient bit is 0. XOR with zeros: 0 0 1 0.

200
00:21:08,515 --> 00:21:20,142
There are no more bits. The remainder is 0 1 0. So R is 0 1 0, and the sender transmits 1 0 1 1 0 1, 0 1 0.

201
00:21:20,592 --> 00:21:28,101
Always check your answer. Divide the 9 transmitted bits by G. The remainder must be zero, and it is.

202
00:21:28,551 --> 00:21:36,103
Now the question bank. Link Layer questions 1 to 5 are short definitions. Pause the video and try them.

203
00:21:36,553 --> 00:21:48,735
Question 1. A link is a communication channel that connects adjacent nodes along the path. Nodes are hosts and routers. A link can be wired, wireless, or a LAN.

204
00:21:49,185 --> 00:21:59,873
Question 2. The services are framing, link access, reliable delivery, flow control, error detection, error correction, and half or full duplex.

205
00:22:00,323 --> 00:22:09,005
Question 3. Framing means: encapsulate the IP datagram in a frame. The link layer adds a header and a trailer.

206
00:22:09,455 --> 00:22:16,730
Question 4. Typical header fields are the destination MAC address, the source MAC address, and the type.

207
00:22:17,180 --> 00:22:23,623
Question 5. The trailer carries the error checking code, for example the CRC bits.

208
00:22:24,073 --> 00:22:31,113
Questions 6 to 9 are about the bit error rate and reliable delivery. Pause the video and try them.

209
00:22:31,563 --> 00:22:44,640
Question 6. The bit error rate is the number of bit errors divided by the number of bits sent. For example, 10 to the power minus 12 means one error per 10 to the power 12 bits.

210
00:22:45,090 --> 00:22:48,909
It is a ratio of bits to bits, so it has no unit.

211
00:22:49,359 --> 00:23:04,484
Question 7. Reliable delivery means frames reach the adjacent node with no bit error, no loss, no duplicate, and in order. The link layer uses sequence numbers, ACKs and timeouts, as in Chapter 3.

212
00:23:04,934 --> 00:23:11,718
Questions 8 and 9 compare the two links. On both links, the CRC catches frame bit errors.

213
00:23:12,168 --> 00:23:19,613
Ethernet drops bad frames and does not retransmit, so frames can be lost. TCP recovers them.

214
00:23:20,063 --> 00:23:24,181
WiFi uses ACKs and timeouts, so frames are not lost.

215
00:23:24,631 --> 00:23:38,199
Both links give no reordering and no duplicates. On Ethernet, one link keeps the order, and with no retransmission there are no duplicates. WiFi uses stop-and-wait and sequence numbers.

216
00:23:38,649 --> 00:23:48,867
Why does Ethernet not use reliable delivery? Errors are very rare, so ACKs cost more than they save. TCP recovers the rare loss.

217
00:23:49,317 --> 00:23:54,821
On WiFi, errors are frequent, so it retransmits on the link, with stop-and-wait.

218
00:23:55,271 --> 00:23:59,261
The starred cells are our inference. They are not on the slides.

219
00:23:59,711 --> 00:24:03,380
Questions 10 to 15. Pause the video and try them.

220
00:24:03,830 --> 00:24:12,619
Question 10. Flow control is pacing between adjacent sending and receiving nodes. The receiver sets the pace of the sender.

221
00:24:13,069 --> 00:24:23,587
Question 11. On a half-duplex link, both ends can send, but not at the same time. On a full-duplex link, both ends can send at the same time.

222
00:24:24,037 --> 00:24:34,661
Question 12. The first way: detect the error and retransmit. WiFi does this on the link. Ethernet drops the frame, and TCP resends.

223
00:24:35,111 --> 00:24:40,081
The second way: correct the error at the receiver, with forward error correction.

224
00:24:40,531 --> 00:24:51,411
Question 13. Error correction means the receiver identifies and corrects the bit errors without a retransmission. 4G and LTE do this.

225
00:24:51,861 --> 00:24:59,285
Question 14. The link layer is in every host and router, in the network interface card or on a chip.

226
00:24:59,735 --> 00:25:08,034
Question 15. It is a combination of hardware, software and firmware. The N I C attaches to the system bus.

227
00:25:08,484 --> 00:25:12,409
Questions 16 to 19. Pause the video and try them.

228
00:25:12,859 --> 00:25:27,494
Question 16. In error detection, the sender computes error detection bits from the data and sends both. The receiver computes the check again and compares. Detection is not 100 percent reliable.

229
00:25:27,944 --> 00:25:39,123
Question 17. The sender adds one parity bit so that the number of 1s is even, or odd. One flipped bit changes the count, so the receiver detects it.

230
00:25:39,573 --> 00:25:44,351
Two-dimensional parity can also find and correct a single bit error.

231
00:25:44,801 --> 00:25:54,273
Question 18, from Chapter 1, slide 15. Guided media: signals propagate in solid media, such as copper and fiber.

232
00:25:54,723 --> 00:25:59,097
Unguided media: signals propagate freely, such as radio.

233
00:25:59,547 --> 00:26:04,965
Chapter 6, slide 19, also names point-to-point and broadcast links.

234
00:26:05,415 --> 00:26:23,229
Question 19. In a CRC, the sender appends r bits R so that the whole pattern is exactly divisible by the generator G, in mod 2 arithmetic. The receiver divides by G. A remainder that is not zero means an error.

235
00:26:23,679 --> 00:26:29,033
To recap. The link layer moves a datagram over one link, inside a frame.

236
00:26:29,483 --> 00:26:38,166
Its services are framing, link access, reliable delivery, flow control, error detection and correction, and the duplex mode.

237
00:26:38,616 --> 00:26:43,800
It is implemented in the network interface card of every host and router.

238
00:26:44,250 --> 00:26:49,946
A parity bit detects one error. Two-dimensional parity also corrects one error.

239
00:26:50,396 --> 00:26:56,625
The Internet checksum adds 16-bit words with wraparound, and then flips all the bits.

240
00:26:57,075 --> 00:27:06,313
A CRC divides by G with XOR, and the remainder is R. The next video covers multiple access protocols.
